#              job monitor — five queues with five status vocabularies and no
#                way to ask "is anything stuck" across them. A UNION ALL, not a
#                shared queue table: the alternative was every writer writing
#                twice forever to populate a read-only page. Read-only by
#                design — each queue has its own retry path, and guessing wrong
#                on an outbox row sends a client a second copy of an email.
#
#              shared inbox (0113) — every query on valuation_comments was
#                keyed by valuation_id. Read state is per (reader, engagement):
#                `last_comment_at` is a property of the thread, unread is a
#                property of the reader, and conflating them gave everyone one
#                badge that went stale at once. Scope is `valuationScope`
#                reproduced in SQL, because filtering after LIMIT hands a
#                partner four rows and calls it page one.
#
#              document categories 6 → 13 (0112) — 0105's six were the finance
#                half, so the charter, bylaws, option plan, board consents, IP
#                schedule and prior 409A all shared `uploads`. Nothing is
#                backfilled out of it: a file filed there was filed by somebody
#                who saw the choices available at the time.
#
#              cap-table structure graph — new. A table cannot show what
#                converts into what or what is paid first; sorted by name it
#                reads "Series A, Series B, Series Seed" while the money goes
#                B, A, Seed. Ranks become columns. It also catches what per-row
#                validation cannot: a stack where half the classes state a
#                seniority is not an invalid row anywhere, and is the usual
#                reason a waterfall pays out in an order nobody intended. Where
#                the conversion target is ambiguous no arrow is drawn — an
#                arrow to a guess reads as a fact. Hand-rolled SVG, because the
#                server already decided the ranks and a force-directed layout
#                would shuffle that ordering into whatever looked balanced.
